    12	# Design analysis for the combo variant
    13	# -------------------------------------
    14	# a) Concrete form and behavior trace
    15	#    - The scene keeps the source-task idiom: a 10x10 grid with blue water (1), bottom-anchored
    16	#      solid walls, and a supported source shelf near one side.
    17	#    - The new visible control is a shared waterline marker in color 8 placed on the two outer
    18	#      basin walls. Those marker cells are ordinary wall cells for support/deletion purposes.
    19	#    - Water still follows the original local motion. The D6 part changes only the resting rule:
    20	#      a moved water pixel may not finish above the marker row, so excess water remains on the
    21	#      source shelf instead of stacking deeper into the basin network.
    22	#    - The D20 part then runs on that limited-fill result: keep only bottom-anchored structure
    23	#      pixels that are 4-neighbor adjacent to final water; erase every other bottom basin pixel.
    24	#    - Small trace:
    25	#         outer walls at columns 2 and 7, divider at column 4, marker row = 6, source on the right.
    26	#         Original task: water fills rows 8, 7, 6, then continues into row 5 and wets more of the
    27	#         upper wall. All wall pixels remain visible.
    28	#         Combo task: once a pixel's best new resting place would be row 5, that move is rejected
    29	#         and the pixel stays on the source shelf. Afterwards, wall cells on rows 2-5 that never
    30	#         touch water are deleted, so the visible basin is truncated to the wetted portion.
    31	#
    32	# b) Complexity calibration
    33	#    - Only one extra symbol is introduced: marker color 8. It always means the waterline and is
    34	#      reused across every sample.
    35	#    - The basin family is deliberately narrow: two adjacent cups with an optional overflow divider.
    36	#      That is enough to expose both parts of the fusion without introducing many unrelated degrees
    37	#      of freedom.
    38	#    - Generator variation comes from source side, divider height, waterline row, source volume,
    39	#      wall tops, and color remapping. The geometry is not so broad that human pattern matching
    40	#      becomes ambiguous.
    41	#
    42	# c) Hidden-assumption review against the source solver
    43	#    - Source settle() assumed every supported background cell was a legal terminal resting place.
    44	#      That fails now: supported cells above the waterline are illegal as new destinations.
    45	#    - Source output checks assumed every water pixel ended with left/down/right blocked. That also
    46	#      fails now because rejected water can stay on the source shelf with air on one side.
    47	#    - D20 adds a post-simulation deletion pass, so returning the settled grid directly is no
    48	#      longer correct. Structure removal must happen after the limited simulation has converged.
    49	#    - The deletion pass must inspect only bottom-anchored structure. The upper source shelf stays
    50	#      untouched; otherwise rejected source water could lose its support.
    51	#    - Combining both changes introduces one more assumption: the marker pixels must belong to the
    52	#      bottom-anchored basin structure, not to a floating guide. The generator guarantees that by
    53	#      placing them on the outer basin walls.
    54	#
    55	# d) Placement legality
    56	#    - The basin uses fixed columns 2 / 4 / 7, so all candidates stay in-bounds and preserve the
    57	#      same non-ambiguous spill geometry as the D6 reference.
    58	#    - The shelf occupies columns 0-2 before optional mirroring. Water is inserted only in cells
    59	#      directly above the shelf, so every source pixel is supported.
    60	#    - The waterline row is always strictly below the source shelf and strictly above the floor.
    61	#      Wall-top choices are always above or equal to that row, so the marker cells are valid wall
    62	#      cells and there are dry wall segments available to delete.
    63	#
    64	# e) Visual observability
    65	#    - In the input, the marker row is explicit and attached to the basin walls. In the output,
    66	#      water visibly stops at that height while some source water remains stranded on the shelf.
    67	#    - The deleted pixels are not arbitrary recolorings: they are precisely the dry wall segments,
    68	#      most visibly the portions above the filled waterline and sometimes an untouched neighboring
    69	#      basin. A human can infer that the waterline controls both fill depth and what survives.
    70	#
    71	# f) Visual cleanliness
    72	#    - Functional layers are limited to background, water, ordinary walls, and marker walls.
    73	#    - The marker is embedded in the wall rather than floating in open space, which keeps the grid
    74	#      readable. Only bottom structure is eligible for deletion, so the upper source scaffold does
    75	#      not fragment across outputs.
    76	#
    77	# g) Change inferability
    78	#    - Color 8 is the only semantic color. Other wall colors are sampled from a small palette but
    79	#      do not encode rules.
    80	#    - The key relation is geometric and repeated across samples: water stops at the shared marked
    81	#      row, and dry wall cells disappear afterwards. No hidden ordering by color id is used.
    82	#
    83	# h) Generator validation / non-degeneration metrics
    84	#    - Each accepted sample must satisfy all of:
    85	#         1. limited-fill output differs from the input,
    86	#         2. limited-fill output differs from the unlimited-fill output,
    87	#         3. the limited-fill output leaves more water on the source shelf than the unlimited one,
    88	#         4. at least two above-waterline basin pixels are deleted, and
    89	#         5. at least two structure pixels are deleted only because of the waterline limit.
    90	#      Metric (5) is the key fusion check: it proves the deletion pattern is not just the plain D20
    91	#      variant applied to an unchanged water simulation.
    92	#
    93	# i) New implicit assumptions and how generate_input() guarantees them
    94	#    - Assumption: all markers share one row. Guaranteed by placing exactly two marker cells on the
    95	#      two outer walls and optionally mirroring the whole scene.
    96	#    - Assumption: rejected water can safely remain at its start cell. Guaranteed by building the
    97	#      source as a compact shelf-supported block on the grid edge.
    98	#    - Assumption: diagonal and horizontal choices stay unique. Guaranteed by the fixed 2/4/7 basin
    99	#      frame and the hand-vetted divider-height templates inherited from the D6 family.
